On $(0, 2)$ the terms of the sine series alternate in pairs of decreasing size, so $c'(x) = -s(x) < 0$: $c$ strictly decreases and has at most one zero there. Grouping terms shows $c(sqrt(2)) > 0$ and $c(sqrt(3)) < 0$, so by the Intermediate Value Theorem $c$ vanishes at some $omega \/ 2 in (sqrt(2), sqrt(3))$. There $s^2(omega \/ 2) = 1 - c^2(omega \/ 2) = 1$, and positivity of $s$ on $(0, 2)$ selects $s(omega \/ 2) = 1$. All shift identities follow from the addition formulae for $s$ and $c$.

For the circle, $gamma(t) = ee^(ii t)$ maps $[0, 2 omega)$ bijectively onto $SS^1$. Given $z = a + ii b$ with $a^2 + b^2 = 1$, strict monotonicity of $c$ on $[0, omega]$ produces $t$ with $c(t) = a$, and $b = s(t)$ or $b = s(2 omega - t)$ according to the sign of $b$, so $gamma$ is surjective; injectivity uses that the least positive period is $2 omega$. Hence

$
  "perimeter of" SS^1 = "length of" gamma = integral_0^(2 omega) abs(gamma'(t)) dif t = integral_0^(2 omega) abs(ii ee^(ii t)) dif t = 2 omega.
$
